    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Oral glutathione has real, modest evidence for evening skin tone and reducing
        pigmentation — not for changing your skin colour.</strong> It works by inhibiting
        tyrosinase, the enzyme that starts melanin production, and by shifting melanin synthesis
        toward the lighter pigment type. The practical points:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Studied dose:</strong> 250mg–500mg daily. A 12-week trial at 250mg/day found measurable reductions in melanin index alongside improved elasticity and wrinkles.</li>
        <li><strong>Timeline:</strong> 4 weeks at the earliest; 8–12 weeks for anything you would notice in a photograph.</li>
        <li><strong>Vitamin C is not optional.</strong> It regenerates glutathione back to its active form, which is why the two are almost always paired.</li>
        <li><strong>Effects are reversible.</strong> Pigmentation returns after you stop, particularly without daily sun protection.</li>
        <li><strong>Skip the IV route.</strong> Intravenous glutathione for skin lightening is not an approved use and has drawn formal regulator safety warnings.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

