      <h2 id="bioavailability">The bioavailability objection</h2>

      <p>
        For years the standard critique was that swallowed glutathione is destroyed in the gut by
        an enzyme called gamma-glutamyltransferase before it can be absorbed. An influential 1992
        study found that a single large oral dose did not raise plasma glutathione, and that
        finding hardened into received wisdom.
      </p>

      <p>
        The picture has since become more nuanced. Single-dose plasma measurements turned out to be
        the wrong endpoint. When Richie and colleagues measured <strong>tissue stores after six
        months of continuous daily dosing</strong>, levels rose significantly. Glutathione status
        behaves like a reservoir that fills slowly, not a spike you can catch in a blood draw an
        hour after a capsule.
      </p>

      <p>
        The practical implication is simple and unglamorous: <strong>daily, continuous, long-term
        dosing</strong> is the only protocol supported by the data. Taking glutathione
        occasionally, or in a two-week burst before an event, has no basis in any of it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="vitamin-c">Why vitamin C is mandatory alongside it</h2>

      <p>
        Glutathione cycles between two states: GSH, the active reduced form, and GSSG, the spent
        oxidised form. Every time it neutralises a free radical it converts to GSSG and becomes
        useless until it is regenerated.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Vitamin C is one of the main agents that performs that regeneration.</strong>
        Without adequate vitamin C, supplemental glutathione oxidises and stalls. With it, the same
        dose keeps cycling.
      </p>

      <p>
        Vitamin C also contributes independently on both fronts that matter here — it is a
        tyrosinase inhibitor in its own right, and it is the non-negotiable cofactor for collagen
        synthesis (covered in detail in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">our guide to collagen for skin</a>). This is why
        <a href="/products/collabloom">Collabloom</a> pairs <strong>250mg of glutathione with 250mg
        of vitamin C from amla extract</strong> rather than treating them as separate products.
      </p>

