      <h2 id="iv-glutathione">Oral vs injectable: the safety difference</h2>

      <p>
        This section matters more than the rest of the article. <strong>Intravenous glutathione for
        skin lightening is not an approved indication anywhere.</strong> It is an off-label
        cosmetic use of an injectable drug, and it has drawn formal safety advisories from
        regulators, including a well-publicised warning from the Philippine FDA following reports
        of serious adverse events.
      </p>

      <p>Documented and reported risks of IV use for cosmetic lightening include:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Severe cutaneous adverse reactions, including Stevens-Johnson syndrome</li>
        <li>Kidney and liver dysfunction at high or repeated dosing</li>
        <li>Thyroid disruption</li>
        <li>Infection and embolism risk from unregulated administration in non-clinical settings</li>
        <li>Unpredictable product quality, since compounded cosmetic preparations are frequently unlicensed</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Oral glutathione at studied doses has a very different profile. It is generally well
        tolerated, has been used in six-month trials without significant adverse effects, and its
        worst realistic outcome is that it does less than you hoped. That trade — a smaller,
        slower, safer effect — is the correct one for a cosmetic goal.
      </p>

