      <h2 id="realistic">What realistic results look like</h2>

      <p>
        Here is what I tell patients, and it sets expectations lower than most marketing does:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Weeks 1–4:</strong> Nothing visible. Glutathione stores are still filling. Any early change you see is hydration from other ingredients.</li>
        <li><strong>Weeks 4–8:</strong> Tone begins to even. Recent marks — a post-acne spot, a patch of sun-triggered pigmentation — fade at the edges first.</li>
        <li><strong>Weeks 8–12:</strong> The measurable window. Overall uniformity improves and skin reads as brighter, mostly because the contrast between pigmented and unpigmented areas has narrowed.</li>
        <li><strong>Beyond 12 weeks:</strong> Maintenance. The effect holds while you continue and reverses over a few months if you stop.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Three things will affect your result more than the brand you buy. <strong>Daily SPF</strong>
        — without it you are producing new pigment faster than glutathione can slow it, and the
        supplement is fighting a losing battle. <strong>Consistency</strong> — the trials dosed
        daily for months. And <strong>the correct diagnosis</strong> — melasma, post-inflammatory
        hyperpigmentation and sun damage respond differently, and melasma in particular usually
        needs topical or procedural treatment alongside anything oral. If pigmentation is your
        primary concern, see a dermatologist before you see a supplement aisle.
      </p>

