      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>How much glutathione should I take daily for skin?</summary>
          <p>
            Published trials showing skin benefits used 250mg to 500mg of oral glutathione per day,
            taken continuously for 4 to 12 weeks or longer. Collabloom provides 250mg per shot,
            paired with 250mg of vitamin C to keep it in its active form.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does glutathione actually lighten skin?</summary>
          <p>
            It reduces melanin production and shifts pigment synthesis toward a lighter melanin
            type, which evens tone and fades recent pigmentation. It does not change your natural
            complexion, and the effect reverses once you stop. Claims of dramatic whitening are not
            supported by the evidence.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long does oral glutathione take to work?</summary>
          <p>
            The earliest measured change in trials was at four weeks, with clearer results at 8 to
            12 weeks. Because oral glutathione fills tissue stores gradually rather than spiking,
            continuous daily dosing is required — short courses have no supporting evidence.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is IV glutathione safer or more effective than oral?</summary>
          <p>
            Intravenous glutathione for skin lightening is not an approved indication and has drawn
            formal regulator safety warnings following reports of serious adverse events, including
            severe skin reactions and kidney complications. Oral dosing at studied levels is
            well tolerated and is the appropriate route for a cosmetic goal.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I take glutathione with collagen?</summary>
          <p>
            Yes, and they complement each other. Glutathione protects existing collagen from
            oxidative degradation while collagen peptides support new synthesis, and both depend on
            vitamin C. Collabloom combines all three in a single 30ml shot.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Are there side effects of oral glutathione?</summary>
          <p>
            Oral glutathione is generally well tolerated at studied doses, and a six-month trial at
            up to 1,000mg daily reported no significant adverse effects. Some people report mild
            gastrointestinal discomfort. Consult a healthcare professional if you are pregnant,
            breastfeeding or taking prescription medication.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

